The queue begins with nodes whose adjacency list is empty. Those are the terminal nodes. Instead of searching forward from every node, the code stores reversed edges so that a newly safe node can notify all nodes that point to it.

`remaining[node]` starts as the original outdegree. It is reduced only when one outgoing destination is already known to be safe. After the queue finishes, scanning the `safe` array gives the required increasing order.

The input graph is not changed. A graph with no terminal nodes, such as a graph made only of cycles, leaves the result empty.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Solution {
    public List<int> EventualSafeNodes(int V, List<int>[] adj) {
        var reversed = new List<int>[V];
        var remaining = new int[V];
        var queue = new Queue<int>();

        for (int node = 0; node < V; ++node) {
            reversed[node] = new List<int>();
            remaining[node] = adj[node].Count;

            if (remaining[node] == 0) {
                queue.Enqueue(node);
            }

            foreach (int next in adj[node]) {
                reversed[next].Add(node);
            }
        }

        var safe = new bool[V];
        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            int node = queue.Dequeue();
            safe[node] = true;

            foreach (int previous in reversed[node]) {
                --remaining[previous];
                if (remaining[previous] == 0) {
                    queue.Enqueue(previous);
                }
            }
        }

        var answer = new List<int>();
        for (int node = 0; node < V; ++node) {
            if (safe[node]) {
                answer.Add(node);
            }
        }
        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.

Space complexity: `O(V + E)`. The extra memory is mainly the reversed adjacency lists.

A node is safe when every neighbor eventually finishes safely. The recursive function records three states: unseen, currently checking, and safe. Reaching a currently checking node means a cycle was found, so that path is rejected.

The result is built by walking through all node numbers in order. This also handles disconnected parts of the graph because DFS starts for every node, not only node zero.

```javascript
class Solution {
    eventualSafeNodes(V, adj) {
        const state = new Array(V).fill(0);

        const dfs = (node) => {
            if (state[node] === 1) {
                return false;
            }
            if (state[node] === 2) {
                return true;
            }

            state[node] = 1;
            for (const next of adj[node]) {
                if (!dfs(next)) {
                    return false;
                }
            }

            state[node] = 2;
            return true;
        };

        const answer = [];
        for (let node = 0; node < V; ++node) {
            if (dfs(node)) {
                answer.push(node);
            }
        }
        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)` because memoized nodes are not explored again.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for `state` and the recursion stack. Terminal nodes are accepted immediately, while a self-loop is detected as unsafe.

A three-state DFS keeps the cycle check small. State `0` means the node has not been checked, `1` means it is on the current DFS path, and `2` means it is safe. Seeing state `1` again means the current path has a cycle.

A node is marked safe only after all of its neighbors are safe. The loop goes in node order, so the returned array is sorted.

Dry run: for `0 -> 1 -> 2` where `2` has no edges, DFS marks `2`, then `1`, then `0` as safe. If `2 -> 1` is added, the edge back to the active node makes `1` and its callers unsafe.

```java
class Solution {
    private boolean isSafe(int node, int[][] adj, int[] state) {
        if (state[node] == 1) {
            return false;
        }
        if (state[node] == 2) {
            return true;
        }

        state[node] = 1;
        for (int next : adj[node]) {
            if (!isSafe(next, adj, state)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        state[node] = 2;
        return true;
    }

    public int[] eventualSafeNodes(int V, int[][] adj) {
        int[] state = new int[V];
        boolean[] safe = new boolean[V];
        int safeCount = 0;

        for (int node = 0; node < V; ++node) {
            if (isSafe(node, adj, state)) {
                safe[node] = true;
                ++safeCount;
            }
        }

        int[] answer = new int[safeCount];
        int index = 0;
        for (int node = 0; node < V; ++node) {
            if (safe[node]) {
                answer[index++] = node;
            }
        }
        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`, since each node and edge is checked a limited number of times.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the state arrays and the recursion stack. A long chain can make the recursion stack large.

This uses DFS with a small memo array. A node starts as `0`, becomes `1` while it is being explored, and becomes `2` after all of its outgoing paths are known to be safe. If DFS reaches a node marked `1`, it has returned to the current path and found a cycle.

The `V` argument is used directly, while `adj[node]` contains the outgoing neighbors. Since nodes are tested in increasing order, no extra sorting is needed.

One edge case is a terminal node: its neighbor loop is empty, so it moves straight to state `2`. A self-loop is also handled because the node is already state `1` when it sees itself.

Dry run for `0 -> 1`, `1 -> []`: checking `0` checks `1` first, marks `1` safe, then marks `0` safe. For a cycle such as `0 -> 1 -> 0`, the second visit to `0` returns false.

```python
class Solution:
    def eventualSafeNodes(self, V, adj):
        state = [0] * V

        def dfs(node):
            if state[node] == 1:
                return False
            if state[node] == 2:
                return True

            state[node] = 1
            for next_node in adj[node]:
                if not dfs(next_node):
                    return False

            state[node] = 2
            return True

        answer = []
        for node in range(V):
            if dfs(node):
                answer.append(node)
        return answer
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the state list and recursive calls. The method does not modify `adj`.

Terminal nodes have no outgoing edges, so they are safe immediately. I reverse every edge and keep the original outgoing count for each node. When a safe node is removed, its predecessors lose one remaining edge. A predecessor becomes safe when that count reaches zero.

The answer is collected by scanning from `0` to `V - 1`, so it is already sorted. The input array is only read and is not changed.

For example, if `4 -> 5` and node `5` is terminal, processing `5` reduces the remaining count of `4` to zero, so `4` is added too.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> eventualSafeNodes(int V, vector<int> adj[]){
        vector<vector<int>> reversed(V);
        vector<int> remaining(V, 0);
        queue<int> pending;

        for (int node = 0; node < V; ++node) {
            remaining[node] = static_cast<int>(adj[node].size());
            if (remaining[node] == 0) {
                pending.push(node);
            }

            for (int next : adj[node]) {
                reversed[next].push_back(node);
            }
        }

        vector<bool> safe(V, false);
        while (!pending.empty()) {
            int node = pending.front();
            pending.pop();
            safe[node] = true;

            for (int previous : reversed[node]) {
                --remaining[previous];
                if (remaining[previous] == 0) {
                    pending.push(previous);
                }
            }
        }

        vector<int> answer;
        for (int node = 0; node < V; ++node) {
            if (safe[node]) {
                answer.push_back(node);
            }
        }
        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.

Space complexity: `O(V + E)` for the reversed graph, counts, queue, and result. An empty graph returns an empty list, and nodes inside a cycle never enter the queue.

